WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.300
How can a guest run

2
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:03.000
directly while the monitor stays

3
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.560
in control? Limited direct execution

4
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.680
lets ordinary guest instructions use

5
00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.660
the processor while sensitive operations

6
00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.860
remain subject to virtualization control.

7
00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.540
Virtualization does not require interpreting

8
00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:16.160
every instruction one by one.

9
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:19.440
Ordinary computation such as arithmetic

10
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.620
can often proceed directly under

11
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.600
the chosen execution environment. Operations

12
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.260
that would change protected machine

13
00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.580
state require a controlled path.

14
00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:30.740
The important distinction is whether

15
00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:32.600
the operation can affect resources

16
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.380
beyond the guest permitted authority.

17
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.860
In the conceptual trap path,

18
00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.360
a sensitive guest operation causes

19
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:40.800
control to pass to the

20
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.280
monitor. The monitor validates or

21
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.420
emulates the permitted effect and

22
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.560
resumes guest execution. Hardware virtualization

23
00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.660
support helps enforce that separation

24
00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:52.300
in suitable implementations.

25
00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.460
The guest kernel remains a

26
00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:56.020
kernel relative to its guest

27
00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.280
applications, yet it is still

28
00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.060
controlled relative to the host.

29
00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.320
Calling a guest privileged therefore

30
00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.480
needs a layer reference. Do

31
00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:05.780
not draw it as freely

32
00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:07.900
rewriting physical host ownership merely

33
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.140
because it runs operating-system code.

34
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.100
Full virtualization aims to support

35
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.120
an unmodified guest using the

36
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:19.040
presented machine interface. Paravirtualization instead

37
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.360
exposes a cooperative interface that

38
00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.580
requires guest awareness or modification.

39
00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:25.540
The distinction is about the

40
00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:27.480
interface contract, not a claim

41
00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.320
that one label always guarantees

42
00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.200
better performance.

43
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.820
Memory names also have layers.

44
00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.320
A guest virtual address belongs

45
00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.320
to a guest process, a

46
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.740
guest physical address describes the

47
00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.520
guest machine view, and a

48
00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.640
host physical address names actual

49
00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:46.280
host storage. Keep these conceptual

50
00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.600
names separate without importing the

51
00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.560
later chapter page-table algorithms.

52
00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.520
A guest may believe a

53
00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.700
resource occupies a particular location

54
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.080
while the monitor maps that

55
00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:57.680
identity onto a different physical

56
00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.360
implementation. That indirection enables controlled

57
00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:03.840
allocation and isolation. A virtual

58
00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:04.940
name is not proof of

59
00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:06.740
a dedicated physical device or

60
00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:08.380
a one-to-one capacity reservation.

61
00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.940
Direct execution supports efficiency, while

62
00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.920
controlled transitions preserve the monitor

63
00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.120
authority. A system can combine

64
00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:18.080
that mechanism with device emulation

65
00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.160
and multiplexed virtual processors. Explain

66
00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:22.580
each operation at its own

67
00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:24.100
layer rather than choosing one

68
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:25.220
word for the whole machine.

69
00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:27.680
For an exam trace, start

70
00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.580
at the guest instruction, identify

71
00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.260
whether it is ordinary or

72
00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.520
sensitive, then show the appropriate

73
00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.460
execution or control-transfer path. State

74
00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:38.820
which layer validates the requested

75
00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:41.740
effect. Keep architectural assumptions visible

76
00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:43.960
because implementations need not use

77
00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:44.860
identical paths.

78
00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:46.580
Things to take note of.

79
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.980
Direct execution and monitor control

80
00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:51.120
can coexist through enforced privilege

81
00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.380
boundaries. Separate full virtualization from

82
00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:57.600
cooperative paravirtualization, and distinguish guest

83
00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.600
virtual, guest physical and host

84
00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:00.340
physical names.
